WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.145
Same family, two different tools: the Link Workstation and the Link Workstand.

00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:08.645
Here is the difference.

00:00:09.245 --> 00:00:13.757
They look similar in a catalogue, so people often order one when they need the other.

00:00:14.037 --> 00:00:18.309
The result is a frame with nowhere to work, or a worktop you never needed.

00:00:18.909 --> 00:00:26.673
A Link Workstation is a self-supporting station with its own worktop, carrying up to 250 kilos UDL.

00:00:26.953 --> 00:00:31.876
A Link Workstand is a freestanding frame for accessories, without its own worktop.

00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:37.528
Use it beside benches, as a tool wall, as a display, or to hold parts in bins.

00:00:37.808 --> 00:00:42.555
Both take the same Link accessories, so they can sit side by side in one line.

00:00:43.155 --> 00:00:48.466
A shirt maker's kitting station is a Link Workstation with four bin rails and a tube light.

00:00:48.746 --> 00:00:55.139
A recycling centre faces Link Workstands toward its dismantling benches to hold sorted parts.

00:00:55.419 --> 00:00:58.763
An electrical brand uses one as a product display.

00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:02.504
So ask one question: does it need its own worktop?

00:01:02.784 --> 00:01:08.720
Comment with your use case, and next, learn rear-justified versus centre-justified frames.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.565
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

